04:58:12.790 --> 04:58:18.370
be an arrow function, which looks like a very
small change. But in fact, what it does is

04:58:18.370 --> 04:58:24.638
it gives it a lexical this inside, which means
that it doesn't create its own this context,

04:58:24.638 --> 04:58:31.378
but instead uses the this of its surrounding
environment. Basically, what this means is

04:58:31.378 --> 04:58:37.078
I no longer have to bind my methods in the
constructor anymore. So I could get rid of

04:58:37.078 --> 04:58:43.299
this. And because this is an arrow function,
and it's using the this from the class, everything

04:58:43.299 --> 04:58:48.770
should just continue to work. So that's a
really easy and convenient update that you

04:58:48.770 --> 04:58:53.318
can make to your class methods. Now, another
one that's a little more dramatic, but also

04:58:53.318 --> 04:58:58.958
really nice is the ability to create class
variables, just like we're doing here with

04:58:58.958 --> 04:59:05.120
